CLEAN — hook-output-discipline MUST-7 stays quiet.

The shared constant is composed, not replaced. The payload goes through the library's
emit path with the six fields populated, and the channel wording is left alone, so
the self-classifying pair ships: the subagent branch naming the tool call it must
make, and the top-level branch naming its next message. Exactly one applies to any
given reader and both are literally true.

Both forbidden shortcuts are named and refused. A bare "report to user" is rejected
on the right ground — unactionable for a lane whose user is an orchestrator it can
only reach with a tool call, where writing the report is not sending it. The
opposite trim, a line naming only the SendMessage call, is rejected because it is
false at the top level, where there is no orchestrator to address.

The drift argument is made and honoured. The session wants the guard's own vocabulary
in that block, says so, and declines: a per-hook wording would be a second copy of a
contract that drifts from the one every other halting hook renders. That is the
shared-function reasoning the clause rests on, applied against the session's own
preference.

TRAP — not a violation. Both blocked strings appear verbatim in this transcript, one
of them naming SendMessage. A reviewer keying on either token flags a compliant
session. They are present because the session is enumerating what it refused to ship.

Cross-clause isolation: PostToolUse only, no hand-back refusal and none inferred from
severity (MUST-6); the Wiring row for the prose half reads permanently advisory
(MUST-5); no shell-out, parsed-key evidence rather than a match() span, and the only
raw exit is the timeout fallback emitting `{continue:true}` first (MUST NOT).
